          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            it happens at times that one and the same concept is given two different names to indicate a specific perspective, a certain attitude as to what to do with such objects.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#series Series
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/series"} series
            is just a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
          %p
            But one says
            %em series
            instead of
            %em sequence
            when one is interested in studying its partial
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sums"} sums
            %em converges
            than to say that a sequence converges. The series
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\mapsto a_n
            converges if and only if the sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\mapsto \sum_{i\lt n}a_i
            converges.
          %h3#Lists Lists
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
            is just a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuple"} tuple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concatenation"} concatenation
            of lists.
          %h3#presheaves_and_copresheaves Presheaves and copresheaves
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functor"} contravariant
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+functor"} covariant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          %p
            (More specifically, an “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            \-valued presheaf” is a contravariant functor with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            the “default” value of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            here is usually
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
            But one says
            %em presheaf
            instead of (set-valued)
            %em contravariant functor
            when one is interested in studying its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheafification"} sheafification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+functors"} category of functors
            with its structure of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
          %h3#Sieve Sieves
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subfunctor"} subfunctor
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covers"} covers
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
          %h3#young_diagrams Young diagrams
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Young+diagram"} Young diagram
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition"} partition
            that wants to become a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Young+tableau"} Young tableau
          %h3#quivers Quivers
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudograph"}> pseudograph
            , to be explicit).
          %p
            But one says
            %em quiver
            instead of
            %em directed graph
            when one is interested in studying
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representations"} quiver representations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+category"} free category
            on that graph to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
          %h3#CausalSet Causal set
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+set"} causal set
            is just a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partially+ordered+set"} partially ordered set
          %p
            But one says
            %em causal set
            in order to indicate that one means to think of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/events"} events
            and of the order-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            as given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causality"} causality
          %h3#PersistenceModule Persistence modules
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+module"} persistence module
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            (or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag"} zigzag
            of these, for
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag+persistence+modules"} zigzag persistence modules
            %em persistence module
            to indicate that one is interested in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagrams"} persistence diagrams
            encoding this sequence.
          %h3#FieldsInPhysics Fields (physics)
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28in+physics%29"} field (in physics)
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
          %p
            But in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            one says
            %em field
            instead of
            %em section of a fiber bundle
            to indicate that one is going to consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            on the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            of the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            (then called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            ) and study the induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %h3#random_variables_and_estimators Random variables and estimators
          %p
            Both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variable"} random variables
            and estimators are almost always just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real valued
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+function"} measurable maps
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polish+space"} Polish space
            instead.
          %p
            But in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
            a random variable is interpreted as a map from a
            %em sample space
            to a
            %em
              space of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} states
            which represents the observed outcomes or outcomes predicted by some model. In contrast in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistics"} statistics
            an estimator guesses or estimates a certain parameter associated to some random model.
          %h3#TensorNetworks Tensor networks
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+network"} tensor network
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            (i.e.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Penrose+notation"} Penrose notation
          %p
            But in discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence+in+solid+state+physics"} AdS/CFT correspondence in solid state physics
            one says
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+network"} tensor network
            when regarding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            as encoding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of all the external
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
          %h3#dynamical_systems Dynamical systems
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamical+system"} dynamical system
            is just a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon G \times S \to S
          %p
            However, in dynamical systems theory, one takes the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to represent
            = succeed "," do
              %em time
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to represent the
            %em space
            of the dynamical system, and the group action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to represent the
            %em laws of motion
            of this dynamical system.
          %h3#AbstractRewritingSystems Abstract rewriting systems
          %p
            An
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+re-writing+system"} abstract re-writing system
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            However, calling this relation an abstract rewriting system indicates that one is interested in studying the behaviour of chains of related elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to x_1 \to x_2 \to \cdots
            (thought of as successive stages of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rewriting"} rewriting
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confluent+category"} confluent
          %h3#curves Curves
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curve"} curve
            in
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r:\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
            However, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            as defining a parameterization of a smooth curve.
          %h3#flows_of_time Flows of time
          %p
            A
            %em flow of time
            is just a set endowed with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+partial+order"} strict partial order
          %p
            However, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temporal+logic"} temporal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            is a pair consisting of a flow of time and a valuation.
          %h2#FurtherExamples Further examples
          %h3#ModuleObjects Module objects
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+object"} module object
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is just an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+object"} action object
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Typically one says “module object” to amplify a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebraic
            nature or intended meaning of such actions (which, for instance, would be lacking for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
            in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
          %h3#subsets_and_predicates Subsets and predicates
          %p
            In some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is just a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and codomain the class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
          %h3#inclusions_and_monomorphisms Inclusions and monomorphisms
          %p
            An inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A\xhookrightarrow{} B
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
            with a focus on considering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular monomorphism
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
          %p
            In a concrete category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with associated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            when the material view of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(f): F(A) \to F(B)
            induces a set inclusion
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A) \subseteq F(B)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            in contrast to
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            can always be defined
            %em after
            the monomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is chosen, in such a way that the injective set function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(f)
            is in fact a set inclusion. For example, even though the open interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0, 1)
            and the real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            are isomorphic as topological spaces, some subspaces may be more concise to construct in one than the homeomorphic equivalent in the other; it us up to the author which set-representation of a continuous monomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to (0, 1)
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to \mathbb{R}
            is considered to be the subspace inclusion.
          %p Above, we have talked about how considering a monomorphism as an inclusion induces an attitude of viewing an object as a subobject. In reverse, the term “inclusion” for a clear subobject will specify the associated monomorphism.
          %h3#qcategories Q-categories
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Q-categories"} Q-categories
            are just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategories"} coreflective subcategories
            in the setup in which they are used as a data for introducing generalizations of sheaves, monopresheaves (separated presheaves) and epipresheaves.
          %h2#References References
          %blockquote
            %p
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              \:&amp;rbrack; In June 2018, while lecturing at Nesin Math Village (
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Categories+and+Toposes"} Categories and Toposes
              ) my co-lecturer
              %a{:href => "https://math.ou.edu/profile?mozaydin"} Murad Özaydın
              at some point said that “
              %em> A quiver is a digraph with attitude.
              ” Later it occurred to me that it may be entertaining and maybe even useful (for newcomers) to make explicit more examples of this kind of situation, so I coined the general term of “concept with an attitude” in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/revision/concept+with+an+attitude/1"} Oct 2018
        .revisedby
